 This is useful when the auto-detection doesn't match the expected grid size. The value must be between 1 and half the smallest image dimension.
 
+`--pixel-size 1` says the picture is already one image pixel per art pixel: it is kept as it is (colours are only reduced if you also pass a colour count or a palette).
+
+#### What the auto-detection does with a picture
+
+- Art that is already on its grid (it has one-pixel details) comes back unchanged, at the same size.
+- Enlarged art (sharp, blurred, noisy, cropped or padded by a few pixels, interpolated, a slightly different horizontal and vertical scale) comes back at one pixel per art pixel.
+- When no grid can be established with enough evidence (a picture with only a handful of edges, a blank or smooth picture), nothing is written, the reason is printed and the exit code is `3`. Pass `--pixel-size N` (or `1`) to decide yourself.
+- `--expect-cell N` states that the result must be a whole number of `N`-pixel cells (for example `32` for sprite sheets). Grids that do not give one are rejected, and a picture whose size is a multiple of `N` with no grid in sight is taken to be on its grid.
+
 Constrain the output to a fixed palette with `--palette`, passing comma-separated hex colors:
 
